    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nA PD decode request never stamps `prefill_finished_time`: its prefill runs on another worker. For non-streaming output, `TokenizerManager` consequently records the arrival of the first internally flushed batch as the first-token time. A speculative step can skip the force-stream boundary, leaving the first batch until completion. We observed `first_token_latency == e2e_latency` for all 17 requests in a DeepSeek-V4-Flash/DSpark NPU run.\r\n\r\n## Change\r\n\r\nRecord a decode-local timestamp when the sampled prefill token is committed. Export it in the existing typed timing report and use it for non-streaming first-token accounting. The scheduler-side decode latency supplied to the Rust frontend uses the same timestamp.\r\n\r\nThe timestamp is written once, survives retraction, and uses the decode worker's clock. It includes the transfer/admission before handoff; it is not the prefill worker's forward-finish time or client receipt time. Keeping it separate leaves prefill phase timing intact. Streaming arrival-based timing, output batching, token generation and metrics-disabled behavior are unchanged. The optional report field is appended so older array payloads retain their default unset value.\r\n\r\n## Validation\r\n\r\nBased on main `e2982985110fb432fd8ddba5f0147a5004fd03ab`. CPU component validation used the actual handoff method, pickle/msgpack timing report, and `TokenizerManager` output handler with a controlled 2-second handoff-to-output delay. The unmodified implementation reports the later arrival (2.0035-second error); the patch records the handoff and retains it through completion.\r\n\r\n114 checks passed: five external component checks plus the existing request-time, tokenizer cleanup, output logprob and output metadata suites. Coverage includes first-write/retraction, absent/disabled metrics, older wire defaults, and the Python scheduler helper used for Rust decode latency. The 14 warnings are existing `torch.jit.script_method` deprecations. Ruff lint/format and `git diff --check` passed. The unchanged baseline implementation was run from `fa5580f6`; the four touched files are identical between that baseline and the main commit above.\r\n\r\nNo post-patch live-model/cross-machine transfer benchmark was run. This fixes accounting, with no model-speed or accuracy claim. The PR contains four production files and no test files.\r\n\r\nThe image below renders selected saved validation output; it is not a terminal screenshot. Image/text live on a separate evidence branch and are outside this PR diff.\r\n\r\n![CPU component validation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AkiYangDev/sglang/8a3e39532138b2e771d518e526e88cd4c696f169/pr-evidence/pd-ttft/validation.png)\r\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #41330859040](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41330859040)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #41330859099](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41330859099)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #41330859047](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41330859047)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Base CI only covers the core cases, and its workload may not exercise your change. Use `/rerun-test` to run the tests your change touches, or copy this prompt for an agent:\n>\n> ```text\n> Find the registered tests this PR affects and run them with /rerun-test, following https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/main/.agents/skills/rerun-test/SKILL.md\n> ```\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
